# apps/api environment. Copy to `.env` (git-ignored) before `pnpm --filter @sam-group/api dev`.

NODE_ENV=development

# Must match API_UPSTREAM in the repository-root .env
# (http://host.docker.internal:3001) — that is what nginx proxies /api/ to in
# development. Changing one without the other produces a 502 from nginx, not an
# error from this application.
API_PORT=3001

# The sam_platform connection, and the only database this application may open
# (ADR-002). A URL naming any other database — sam_cms above all — is rejected at
# startup by config/env.validation.ts, so the process refuses to boot rather than
# failing at the first query.
#
# This DUPLICATES DATABASE_URL in the repository-root .env, which is where the
# Prisma CLI and prisma/seed.ts read it from. Both must carry the same value; the
# root file documents the four compose variables it is assembled from. Nothing
# expands ${VAR} inside a .env file, so keep them in sync by hand.
#
# Note that @nestjs/config reads `.env` relative to the working directory, so this
# file applies when the application is started from apps/api.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://sam_platform_user:local-dev-only-change-me@localhost:5432/sam_platform?schema=public

# --- Payload CMS (Content module) -------------------------------------------
#
# The two values below are how the Content module reaches Payload, server-to-
# server on the internal network (ADR-003, API_CONTRACT_FINAL.md §4). They are
# read ONLY by apps/api. `apps/web` must never be given either of them: the
# frontend has no awareness Payload exists, and a CMS origin in a browser bundle
# would advertise a surface the architecture says is not there.
#
# BOTH ARE OPTIONAL, unlike DATABASE_URL above. With either missing, the API
# still boots and every other endpoint works; `GET /content/*` answers 503
# UPSTREAM_UNAVAILABLE and logs why. That is deliberate — taking the catalog,
# blog and forms down because one module's upstream is unconfigured would be a
# worse failure than the one being reported.
#
# In development apps/cms runs on the host via `pnpm dev` and listens on 3002,
# matching CMS_UPSTREAM in the repository-root .env. In production it is a
# container behind the same nginx and this becomes the compose service name.
# ORIGIN ONLY — no path; the /api prefix is Payload's and lives in the client.
PAYLOAD_INTERNAL_URL=http://localhost:3002

# The API key of a Payload user whose role is `service` — an identity of its own,
# never an editor's login and never an end user's token. Payload constrains that
# role to PUBLISHED documents in its own access control, so this credential
# cannot read a draft even if this application asked for one.
#
# HOW TO OBTAIN ONE: sign in to Payload's admin UI, open the Users collection,
# create a user whose only role is `service`, tick "Enable API Key", generate the
# key, and paste it here. It is a REAL SECRET — never commit it, never reuse one
# environment's key in another, and treat a leaked key as read access to every
# published page. Left empty here so a copied file cannot carry a working one.
PAYLOAD_API_KEY=
